  "throughline": "In the summer of 1714, an old and out-of-favor [Leibniz](/philosophy/thinker/leibniz) sat down in Vienna and wrote, in French, a condensed map of his entire philosophy: ninety short numbered paragraphs that start from a single question and never let go of it. The question is what the world is ultimately made of. His answer is that it is made of simple substances (self-standing basic things that exist in their own right, not as qualities or parts of something else), partless and immaterial, that he calls monads, and that these monads have no windows, meaning nothing ever enters or leaves one, so that nothing in the universe ever truly acts on anything else. From that one refusal the book builds outward by strict steps: every monad changes only from within; every monad perceives the whole universe from its own point of view, the way one town looks like many towns seen from many sides; two great principles govern all reasoning, contradiction and sufficient reason; sufficient reason, pushed to the end, forces a necessary being, which is God; God surveys infinitely many possible worlds and produces the best; soul and body keep perfect time without ever touching, like two clocks synchronized at the creation; and rational souls form a moral community, a City of God. He gave the text no title and never published it. The name *Monadology* was added by a German editor six years later, after Leibniz was dead. What survives is the tightest statement he ever made of the strangest system in early modern philosophy, an argument that the world's ultimate constituents are sealed, perceiving points, each one alone, all in perfect agreement.",
